Communication Overview

Three unique IP addresses were identified during the packet capture.

IP Address	Observation
192.168.0.114	Internal private host
192.168.0.1	Local network device (possibly the default gateway)
72.14.207.99	Remote public host

Based solely on the observed packet flow, it is assumed that:

192.168.0.114 is the client initiating ICMP Echo Requests.
192.168.0.1 appears to be a local network device, possibly the default gateway. This cannot be confirmed from the packet capture alone.
72.14.207.99 appears to be the remote destination responding to the ICMP requests.

Since this packet capture was downloaded from the Internet, these assumptions are made only from the communication pattern and cannot be verified without additional network information.

ICMP Type Analysis

Only two ICMP message types were observed.

Type 8 – Echo Request

Echo Request packets are generated to verify whether a destination host is reachable across the network.

Observed Characteristics

Type = 8
Code = 0
Identifier = 768 (0x0300)
Payload present
Valid checksum
Matching sequence numbers

These packets represent connectivity requests sent by the initiating host.

Type 0 – Echo Reply

Echo Reply packets are generated by the destination host after successfully receiving an Echo Request.

Observed Characteristics

Type = 0
Code = 0
Identifier matches request
Sequence number matches request
Payload matches request
Valid checksum

Every Echo Request received a corresponding Echo Reply, confirming successful communication.

