Limitations

Several conclusions are based on observations rather than confirmed network information.

The role of 192.168.0.1 as the default gateway is assumed based on communication patterns and cannot be verified from the packet capture alone.
TTL values provide only an estimate of hop count and possible operating system defaults.
Since the PCAP was obtained from an external source, the actual network topology is unknown.
Conclusion

The captured ICMP traffic represents normal network connectivity testing using ICMP Echo Requests and Echo Replies.

Sixteen ICMP packets were successfully exchanged between three observed IP addresses. Every Echo Request received a corresponding Echo Reply, with matching Identifiers and Sequence Numbers, confirming successful end-to-end communication. All packets contained valid checksums, the payload was correctly echoed, and no packet loss or transmission errors were detected. Only ICMP Type 8 (Echo Request) and Type 0 (Echo Reply) messages with Code 0 were present, indicating standard ICMP operation.

Based on the observed communication pattern, one host appears to function as the client, another as a local network device (possibly the default gateway), and the third as a remote Internet host. This interpretation remains an assumption because the capture alone does not provide sufficient evidence to definitively identify device roles.

Overall, the packet capture demonstrates healthy ICMP communication with no evidence of packet corruption, network failures, or malicious ICMP activity. From a SOC analyst perspective, this traffic is consistent with legitimate network reachability testing and does not indicate suspicious behavior.
